## Deep Dive: Semaphore

### What is a Semaphore?
A concurrency primitive that limits access to a shared resource.
Think of it like a parking lot with limited spaces.

```
Parking Lot (semaphore with capacity 3):

[Car1] [Car2] [Car3]   ← 3 cars parked (slots full)

New car arrives → MUST WAIT until a car leaves
```

### Semaphore in Go (buffered channel)
```go
// Create semaphore with capacity 3
semaphore := make(chan struct{}, 3)

// Acquire (take a slot) - blocks if full
semaphore <- struct{}{}

// Release (free a slot)
<-semaphore
```

### Visual Example
```
Initial: semaphore = [_, _, _]  (3 empty slots)

Acquire: semaphore <- struct{}{}
         semaphore = [x, _, _]  (1 slot taken)

Acquire: semaphore <- struct{}{}
         semaphore = [x, x, _]  (2 slots taken)

Acquire: semaphore <- struct{}{}
         semaphore = [x, x, x]  (FULL!)

Acquire: semaphore <- struct{}{}
         ↓
         BLOCKS! Waits until a slot is freed.

Release: <-semaphore
         semaphore = [x, x, _]  (1 slot freed)
         ↓
         Blocked goroutine can now proceed!
```

### Two Channels, Two Purposes

| Channel | Purpose | When Acquired | When Released |
|---------|---------|---------------|---------------|
| `conns` | Store **idle** connections | Acquire from pool | Release to pool |
| `semaphore` | Track **total** connections | Create new conn | Destroy conn |

### Why Not Just Check `len(conns)`?
```go
// WRONG: Race condition!
if len(conns) < maxSize {
    // Multiple goroutines can pass this check simultaneously
    conn := factory()  // Creates more than maxSize!
}
```

The semaphore provides **atomic** slot acquisition:
```go
// RIGHT: Atomic slot acquisition
select {
case semaphore <- struct{}{}:  // Only 1 goroutine succeeds
    conn := factory()           // Guaranteed under limit
default:
    // Wait for slot
}
```

